23.2 Principles of Sustainable Steel Construction
a) The structural steel design should provide a structure capable of fulfilling its intended function while sustaining the specified loads for its intended life with due regard to sustainability, robustness, economy, health and safety. Any expected change in the intended function during its life time should also be considered;
b) In environmentally sustainable construction all stake holders should consider the broader and holistic implications of the decision, which include the whole life cycle sustainability, impacts on other parts of the building and the project, externalities, maintenance, societal cost and consequences, without impinging upon safety and efficiency of the structure;
c) All parties in the supply chain for structural steelwork should take practical steps to reduce the greenhouse gas emissions of the processes over which they have control, thus contributing to sustainable construction.
Further, construction operations should avoid/minimize polluting the environment with dust, wastewater, chemicals and leaching debris;
d) Steelmaking should make full use of available secondary material (scrap) globally;
e) Structural steelwork should be designed, detailed, and constructed to minimize material use and wastage of material and facilitate its recovery for reuse at its end-of-life, as a part of the circular economy, to the extent that this is reasonably practicable; and f) The design should consider practical aspects of construction to avoid repair and rework.

23.3.1 Requirements from Employer
a) Appointment of key contractors (Architects, contractors for Steel work, cladding, glazing, Mechanical, Electrical, coating systems, etc) as early as practical to ensure decisions are fully coordinated;
b) Maximization of structural zones that facilitate lean and efficient designs; and c) Flexibility in design, if use of reclaim materials is considered.

23.3.3 Requirements from the Engineering Group
a) Design for material efficiency, equipment efficiency, and minimal embodied energy and longevity;
b) Consider holistically the environmental impact implication of temporary works and supports;
c) Design with inbuilt flexibility and adaptability;
d) Consider the economic and environmental implications of the type of connections chosen; and e) Assess the requirement of protective treatment against corrosion and fire to minimize the life time maintenance and avoid over-specification.

23.4.2 Minimizing Material Consumption through Design
Minimizing material consumption through design may be achieved by:
a) Avoiding over-specification of design loads;
b) Optimizing spans and grids: and adapting the building layout to take full advantage of the structural steelwork’s attributes;
c) Minimizing complex load paths and transfers in order to maximize direct load paths to ground;
d) Ensuring that structural deflection and vibration criteria, coordination zones and member depths are appropriate and allow for efficient structural solutions that avoid excessive material use;
e) Aiming to design for utilizations as close to 1.0 as practically possible, with due regard to permanent and temporary conditions;
f) Specifying higher strength steel grades in members or part of members not governed by serviceability criteria;
g) Considering key load paths, anticipated connection types and accurate loading criteria, in order to facilitate lean design principles;
h) Avoiding the over rationalization of steel member sizes to ensure efficient use of material, with due consideration of minimum economic quantities for procurement;
j) Reviewing section sizes and minimizing weight, whilst complying with safe methods of erection and fire protection requirements; and k) Semi-continuous design, which assumes that the joints transfer moments from the beam to the column and allow rotation between the connected members as the load is resisted.
NOTE — Historically, steelwork design and fabrication has mainly been driven by cost however, sustainability and particularly the climate emergency, are likely to change this with more clients willing or mandated to pay more for sustainable and low carbon buildings.
Reducing the carbon impact of steel structures through lean and less conservative design is technically achievable but can require more design effort and more complex fabrication.
