ANNEX E
(Foreword and Clause 5) CRITERIA FOR STRUCTURAL SAFETY OF TALL CONCRETE BUILDINGS E-1  SCOPE E-1.1  This Annex covers the following design aspects of reinforced concrete (RC) buildings of height greater than 50 m, but less than or equal to 250 m. Generally, these requirements are based on prescriptive approach and covers the following design aspects of tall buildings:
a) Selection of appropriate structural system;
b) Geometric proportioning of the building;
c) Integrity of structural system;
d) Resistance to wind and earthquake effects; and e) Other special considerations related to tall buildings.
PART C STRUCTURAL DESIGN — SECTION 5 STRUCTURAL CONCRETE

E-1.2  This Annex is not applicable for tall buildings located in the near-field of seismogenic faults.  For the purposes of this standard, near-field is taken as 10 km (shortest distance) from a seismogenic fault.
E-1.3  This Annex is applicable only for buildings that house 20 000 or fewer persons.
E-1.4  This Annex may also be used for design of buildings of height equal to or less than 50 m. The provisions of this Annex will add value to the design of such buildings.  However, this Annex is not applicable for design of buildings of height more than 250 m.
E-1.5  This Annex addresses the following typical structural systems of tall concrete buildings:
a) Structural wall systems;
b) Moment frame systems;
c) Moment frame — Structural wall systems;
d) Structural wall — Flat slab floor systems with perimeter moment frame;
e) Structural wall — Framed tube systems;
f) Framed-tube system;
g) Tube-in-tube system;
h) Multiple tube system;
j) Hybrid system; and k) Any of the above with additional framing systems, for example, outrigger trusses, belt trusses and braced frames.
E-1.6  This Annex shall be read along with all Indian Standards relevant to design and construction of buildings and structures.  In case of conflict, clauses given in this Annex shall be applicable.
E-1.7  For buildings that do not conform to the prescriptive requirements of this Annex, a more rigorous procedure is necessary for design and review.
E-2 - E-3 TERMINOLOGY For the purpose of this Annex, the following definitions shall apply.
E-3.1 Building Height — The difference in levels between the base level of a building, at which inertia forces generated in the structure are transferred to the foundation, which then transfers these forces to the ground, and the highest level of a building, which is the roof of the topmost level which is structurally integral and continuous with the floor below (see also E-4).
E-3.2 Connecting Structure — A structure that links two or more towers, except at the podium levels.
NOTE — There are two distinctive types of connecting structures with each one requiring a different structural treatment, namely, (a) the connecting structure having the role of transferring gravity loads only and with no participation in the role of resisting the lateral loads on either of the towers; and (b) the connecting structure participating in the lateral load resisting structural system, where the two towers act together when one or both towers is subjected to lateral loads due to the interaction provided by the connecting tower.
E-3.3 Core — An assembly of structural walls coupled with link elements and offering lateral stiffness in two orthogonal directions.
E-3.4 Coupled Structural Wall Building — A building comprising of structural walls linked by in-plane beam elements, wherein the vertical and lateral loads are resisted by these structural walls, through axial load, in-plane bending moment and shear force, and the coupling action created by push-pull in the wall elements by framing action provided by link beams.
E-3.5 Frame Building — A building comprising beams and columns, wherein the vertical and lateral loads are resisted by these elements through moment-frame action.
