National Building Code Sectional Committee, CED 46 FOREWORD This NBCS (Part C/Subsection 6A) covers the structural design aspects of steel structures in buildings.
This Section covers the use of hot-rolled structural steel sections and steel tubes in buildings.  It covers the design by limit state method and plastic theory.
This Section was first published in 1970 and revised in 1983, 2005 and 2016 as a chapter of the National Building Code of India.  In the second revision of 2005, the detailed design provisions were not included as IS 800 : 1984 ‘Code of practice for general construction in steel (second revision)’ was under revision at that time. This Section, therefore, then referred to the 1984 version of IS 800 with the remarks that the latest version would prevail, as and when published.  It, however, covered the philosophical aspects of limit state design which was being introduced in the revision of IS 800. IS 800 was subsequently revised and published as IS 800 : 2007 and hence, became the prevailing version for use under this Section.  In the 2016 version of the Code, this Section was brought it in line with the revised version of IS 800 : 2007 read with its Amendment No. 1 and to take care of further improvements and incorporations required.
In the current revision, this publication has been renamed as National Building Construction Standards (NBCS).
This revision of the Section is intended to bring it in line with the revised version of IS 800 : 2026 (under publication) to take care of further improvements and incorporations required. In this revision, therefore, the following major modifications have been made:
a) The working stress design has been discontinued due to the increasing familiarity of designers with the limit state design of steel structures;
b) The table on tensile properties of structural steel products has been updated by including the latest Indian Standards relating to structural steels and aligning the corresponding provisions;
c) Fire-resistant steel as per IS 15103 : 2002 ‘Fire resistant steel — Specification’, structural weatherresistant steels as per IS 11587 : 1986 ‘Structural weather resistant steels — Specification’, and structural steels for buildings and structures with improved seismic resistance as per IS 15962 : 2012 ‘Structural steels for buildings and structures with improved seismic resistance — Specification’ have been included in the table of tensile properties of structural steel products;
d) Provisions related to the requirement of temperature stress analysis have been updated;
e) Provisions related to the requirement of fire load have been updated;
f) Table 2 Limiting width thickness ratio of elements has been updated;
g) Provisions related to the consideration of second order effects have been updated;
h) Provisions related to design for earthquake loads relating to load and load combinations, various types of frames and connections in the critical zones of frames have been updated;
j) Provisions related to bracing connections have been updated;
k) Provisions related to fabrication procedures have been updated;
m) Certain terminologies, symbols and figures have been updated based on the changes incorporated;
n) Buckling class of cross section table has been updated;
p) Imperfection parameter for lateral torsional buckling of beam has been added;
q) A new clause on sustainable steel construction has been added. This provision covers the following:
1) Requirements from Engineers, contractors and supply chain members;
2) Sustainable design process by minimizing the materials, fabrication; and avoidance of maintenance;
