23.5.1 Information Records
A non-exhaustive checklist of information to be communicated in sustainable fabrication are given below:
a) Sustainability information to be provided to the steelwork contractor:
1) any part of the steelwork that is to incorporate reclaimed steel products;
2) any part of the steelwork that is to be left untreated;
3) any details of the fire design, namely the design utilization per section size in the fire condition;
4) any workshop waste targets;
5) a life cycle assessment of the whole building; and 6) any specific material sourcing requirements.
b) Sustainability information to be provided by the steelwork contractor:
1) All relevant EPDs for the steel and other products;
2) The embodied carbon of the fabricated steelwork;
3) Digital records of the ‘As erected’ structure, which should include all condition of structures, survey, metadata, etc;
4) Material quality and quantity data, including batch traceability for main structural members to facilitate future reuse (to be included in the operation and maintenance manual; and 5) Documentation of evidence of an active environmental management system.

23.5.2 Fabrication Waste Management
Strategies for fabrication waste management include:
a) ordering directly from mills to minimize offcuts;
b) using stock lengths as ordered efficiently to minimize waste. Where possible, splice locations in steel members should be coordinated to fall within standard stock length sizes;
c) recycling and reusing steel fabrication waste, for example, off-cuts, swarf, etc;
d) considering use off-cuts of material, particularly from plate, elsewhere, for example, temporary works, connection fittings, shims, packs, rather than simply scrapping for recycling. Always prioritized reuse over recycling;
e) considering use of machines that facilitate common line cutting of fittings;
f) minimizing coating waste and coating packaging waste, by using intermediate bulk; and g) Minimizing/ avoiding overspray of coatings should ensure operators are properly trained and the appropriate and properly maintained equipment is used.
NATIONAL BUILDING CONSTRUCTION STANDARDS 2026

23.5.3 Sustainable Welding
Strategies for sustainable welding include:
a) Avoiding low productivity processes and using automatic and semi-automatic processes with continuous wire feed, thus using material efficiently and avoiding energy waste;
b) Adopting energy-saving processes such as, inverter-based power sources instead of older regulator/rectifier-based sources, electrically powered pre-heating with temperature control instead of gas flame;
c) Minimizing pre-heat in the welding procedure specification and welding continuously to avoid the need to re-heat; and d) Periodically reviewing welding techniques and weld procedures to make the best use of the latest advancements in welding equipment and consumables, including the selection of joint set-ups and consumables that allow for deeper penetration fillet welds to be achieved, thus potentially reducing the number of weld runs required.

24.13.4.4 In addition to precautions against all the hazards mentioned above, erection workers shall also be
protected in the following manner:
a) all workers shall wear helmets and shall also be provided with gloves and shoes. In addition those working at heights shall use safety belts;
b) all structures shall be so braced/guyed during erection that there is no possibility of collapse before erection work is completed; and c) warning signs such as ‘DANGER’, ‘CAUTION’, ‘440 V’, ‘DO NOT SMOKE’, ‘LOOK AHEAD’, etc;
should be displayed at appropriate places.

