5.6.1.1 Hardware refers to items:
a) To be placed on or in the structure to receive the precast concrete units (anchor bolts, angles, or plates with suitable anchors);
b) To be embedded in the concrete units for connection or for other mechanical, plumbing, glazing, miscellaneous iron, masonry, roofing works, etc; and c) Necessary for the installation of the precast concrete units.

5.7.1.2 Relevant manufacturer’s guidelines shall be used for selecting the appropriate product. In addition to that,
the following points shall be considered:
a) The box and the two flexible wire loops shall be made of carbon steel sheet and high strength wire ropes, respectively. The surface of the box shall be equipped with a profiled surface to ensure sufficient bonding with the surrounding concrete;
b) Proper selection involves ensuring compliance with tensile strength requirements and alignment accuracy to prevent misfits. High-strength, non-shrink non-metallic cementitious grout is recommended to encapsulate loops fully. Care shall be taken in application to maintain uniform joint appearance, especially in exposed architectural elements;
c) The looped wire ropes shall not react with grout and form corrosion. Also, shall not be used if found having corrosion; and d) The manufacturer shall furnish the detailed technical datasheet along with the test results corroborating the values noted in the technical datasheet. Also, the same shall be verified by the engineer-in-charge by conducting appropriate testing per batch.

6.2.1 Structural Concept
Precast concrete structural systems shall be explicitly designed to ensure continuity between elements and robustness of the overall system. The selected system shall be robust and shall be designed to withstand the following:
a) Stability against progressive collapse;
PART C  STRUCTURAL DESIGN — SECTION 7 PREFABRICATED CONCRETE CONSTRUCTION
b) Prevention of structural failure under all design load combinations;
c) Fire resistance as per applicable regulations;
d) Adequate ductility and energy dissipation capacity;
e) Redundancy and continuity in load paths;
f) Control of cracking within serviceability limits;
g) Acceptable deformation within serviceability limits; and h) Durability against environmental exposure.

6.2.1.1 The structural system and each of its components shall maintain stability during all stages, including
transportation, erection and service life. The following factors shall be considered for the selection of precast system:
a) Grid distances;
b) Spanning direction of slab and beam;
c) Position of columns and walls;
d) Position of load bearing walls and/or facade and/or facade at stilt;
e) Position and approximate requirements for stabilizing elements; and f) Position of expansion joints.

6.2.2 Standardization of Precast Components/Elements
The precast components/elements shall preferably be standardized based on the structural, architectural and/or aesthetic requirements. The precast components/elements given below but not limited to, shall be used in accordance with relevant Indian Standards (see Part B ‘Building Materials’ of this NBCS) and the accepted standards, where available:
a) Reinforced/prestressed concrete channel unit;
b) Reinforced/prestressed concrete slab unit;
c) Reinforced/prestressed concrete beams;
d) Reinforced/prestressed concrete columns;
e) Reinforced/prestressed concrete hollow core slab;
f) Reinforced concrete waffle slab/shells;
g) Reinforced/prestressed concrete wall elements;
h) Hollow/solid concrete blocks and battens;
j) Precast planks and joists for flooring and roofing;
k) Precast joists and trussed girders;
m) Light weight/cellular concrete slabs/wall panels;
n) Precast lintels and Chajjas;
p) Large panel prefabricates;
q) Reinforced/prestressed concrete trusses;
r) Reinforced/prestressed roof purlins;
s) Precast concrete L-panel units;
t) Prefabricated concrete double-T units;
